Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Every external library gets a verdict: what it is, how it
 is integrated, and whether more would help
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The tree carries 44 CPM declarations, 12 vendored sources, a JUCE submodule and
three discovered maths SDKs, and the records disagreed about several of them, so
the audit starts from the link line and the pins rather than from the older
docs. Each component is classified by the pattern it actually uses - submodule,
vendored plus CPM fallback, configured subproject, download-only with a local
target, discovered SDK, dev-only - and then judged on whether moving it deeper
would buy anything.

The finding is that the split is already correct: the vendored set is the code
the harnesses must compile both ways, the CPM set is the code whose upstream
module registration makes a fork worse than a fetch, and the unlinked set is
alternatives to capabilities the binary already has or references to another
host. The one genuinely open piece is the staged sokol/bgfx renderer scene, which
is feature work rather than dependency work. Licence boundaries are recorded
where they decide the question: libsndfile, chowdsp_dsp_utils, aubio, Rack and
cycfi_infra stay off the link line for reasons that are legal before technical.

